BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Warren Wilson College
DTSTAMP:20261010T053030Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54047270730023
DTSTART:20261007T230000Z
DTEND:20261008T013000Z
DESCRIPTION:Time & Location\n\nOct 07\, 2026\, 7:00 PM – 9:30 PM\n\nKittr
 edge Theatre\, Warren Wilson College\, 701 Warren Wilson Rd\, Swannanoa\, 
 NC 28778\, USA\n\nAbout the Event\n\nRSVP HERE\n\nAn evening with Stephen 
 Jenkinson and Kimberly Ann Johnson\n\nHosted by Aditi Sethi\n\n \n\nWednes
 day\, October 7\, 2026 \n\n7:00 to 9:00 pm conversation\n\n9:00 to 9:30 pm
  book signing\n\n \n\nSome books are written from a distance. This one was
  not.\n\n \n\nIn January of 2024\, Stephen Jenkinson received a diagnosis 
 of Parkinson's disease and began keeping a journal. He had spent decades w
 alking alongside dying people and their families\, first as director of pa
 lliative care at a Toronto hospital and later through the Orphan Wisdom Sc
 hool. Now the country he had guided so many others through had arrived at 
 his own door.\n\n \n\nWhat he wrote across that first year became Tremblin
 g\, Still: The Awful Clarity of a Mind in Eclipse. It is a dated chronicle
  rather than a memoir\, written in real time\, with no benefit of hindsigh
 t and no idea yet how the year would end. He has said that he did not so m
 uch write the book as panic it onto the page\, and that he had to invent w
 ords to carry what the ordinary ones could not. There is a glossary of the
 m in the back. What began in devastation opened\, unexpectedly\, into myst
 ery\, and into a kind of mercy that did not depend on hope.\n\n \n\nMuch o
 f it was written while Stephen was staying with Kimberly Ann Johnson\, his
  friend and the co-author of Reckoning. On the evening of October 7 they s
 tep back into that time in person\, on stage\, here in Swannanoa.\n\n \n\n
 The evening is a conversation between the two of them. Aditi Sethi\, hospi
 ce physician and Emberlight's founder\, will welcome Stephen and Kimberly 
 to the stage\, open the evening\, and hold its shape. Then Stephen and Kim
 berly take the conversation from there\, in front of us\, for two hours. A
 fterward they will be signing books and available for brief exchanges.\n\n
  \n\nThey will talk about the experience around making this book\, and how
  it has evolved since it was captured on the page in that moment in time. 
 What was happening in the rooms around the writing\, and what did not make
  it onto the page. What has held true in the two years since\, and what ha
 s quietly fallen away. They will look at that season from where they stand
  now\, and let us listen in.\n\n \n\nAt Emberlight\, we practice ways to e
 mbrace death and support one another with loving presence. Deep listening\
 , compassion\, and bearing witness are how we meet people at the bedside\,
  and they are what this evening asks of all of us in the room. This is not
  a lecture about mortality. It is a chance to sit in the company of someon
 e speaking plainly from inside his own undoing\, alongside a friend who wa
 s there for the writing of it\, and to let that honesty do what honesty do
 es.\n\n \n\nYou can purchase a copy of Trembling\, Still online here if yo
 u would like to read ahead\, and bring it with you to be signed. Stephen a
 nd Kimberly will also have copies available at the event.\n\n \n\nWhere an
 d when\n\nWe gather at the Kittredge Theatre in the Kittredge Arts Center 
 at Warren Wilson College\,\n\n701 Warren Wilson Rd\, Swannanoa\, NC 28778.
 \n\n \n\nDoors open at 6:30 pm. The conversation runs from 7:00 to 9:00 pm
 \, followed by the book signing until 9:30 pm.\n\n \n\nAbout Stephen Jenki
 nson\n\nStephen Jenkinson is a culture activist and ceremony man\, a pract
 itioner of the eloquence of tongue and hand. He is an author\, storyteller
 \, musician\, sculptor\, raconteur\, and off grid organic farmer\, and has
  published seven books over a public life spent advocating for conscious l
 iving and dying. He worked extensively with dying people and their familie
 s\, formerly serving as director of palliative care at Mount Sinai Hospita
 l of Toronto. He is the creator of the Orphan Wisdom School\, cofounded wi
 th his wife Nathalie Roy in 2010\, and co-founder of the Nights of Grief a
 nd Mystery with singer songwriter Gregory Hoskins. He holds a master's deg
 ree in theology from Harvard University and a master's of social work from
  the University of Toronto. In 2023 Harvard honored him with a Distinguish
 ed Alumni Award for helping people navigate grief and connecting humanity 
 through ceremony and storytelling. He is the subject of the documentary Gr
 iefwalker\, and author of the award winning Die Wise.\n\n \n\nAbout Kimber
 ly Ann Johnson\n\nKimberly Ann Johnson is a somatic practitioner\, postpar
 tum advocate\, and culture worker. She studied yoga directly with the thre
 e main lineage holders of the Krishnamacharya tradition and taught for fif
 teen years while running a Structural Integration practice. When her life 
 was rearranged by childbirth\, she turned toward the cultural chasm around
  postpartum care and trained in Somatic Experiencing and Sexological Bodyw
 ork to support women through birth injury\, gynecological surgery\, and se
 xual boundary violation. She is the author of Call of the Wild and the ear
 ly mothering classic The Fourth Trimester\, and co-authored Reckoning with
  Stephen Jenkinson. Her next book\, Erotic Seasons: Tuning into the Rhythm
 s of Women's Sexuality and Spirituality\, arrives in November from St. Mar
 tin's Press. She hosts the Sex Birth Trauma podcast and is the steward of 
 Mothering the Bones.\n\n \n\nAbout Aditi Sethi\, MD\n\nDr. Aditi Sethi is 
 a hospice and palliative care physician\, end of life doula\, musician\, a
 nd the founder and Executive Director of Emberlight: Center for Conscious 
 Living & Dying. Her accompaniment of Ethan Sisser in the final weeks of hi
 s life is documented in the film The Last Ecstatic Days\, and the question
  Ethan asked her then\, whether dying might be a communal experience rathe
 r than a solitary one\, became the seed of this organization. She is an em
 erging and important voice for shifting our culture's approach to dying\, 
 death\, and grief tending.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\nThis is a Pay What You C
 an event\, and all proceeds support Emberlight. The care we offer is free 
 to every resident and family who comes to us\, and it stays free because t
 his community holds it up. Your ticket goes directly into that work. We in
 vite you to give what feels right for you\, and if you are able to give be
 yond the suggested amount\, that generosity is what keeps a bed open and a
  hand held for the next person who needs one. We would love to welcome you
  into this circle whatever the amount.\n\n \n\nPlease note that all event 
 tickets are non-refundable\, except in the case that Emberlight cancels th
 e event.\n\n \n\nPlease contact events@emberlightway.org with any question
 s.
GEO:35.613818;-82.438862
LOCATION:Kittredge Arts Center\, Kittredge Theater
SUMMARY:Trembling\, Still: The Awful Clarity of a Mind in Eclipse
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.warren-wilson.edu/event/trembling-still-the-aw
 ful-clarity-of-a-mind-in-eclipse
CATEGORIES:Lecture
CATEGORIES:Performance
